	abstract = {β€” The rising level of sophistication, frequency of cyber attacks on critical infrastructure that has been recorded in recent years has underscored the urgent necessity of real-time, secure, and transparent sharing of cyber threat intelligence (CTI). The centralized sharing scheme also has problems of scalability, trust management, and tolerance to single failure points. This paper presents an automated blockchain-based decentralized intrusion detection network (DIDN) implemented using smart contracts for CTI sharing among critical infrastructure nodes. Utilizing blockchain's irrevocable nature, consensus by distributed nodes, and trust through smart contracts, the framework ensures that threat intelligence is verifiable, immutable, and accessible to trusted parties without trust in a single party. Each node that is a part of the infrastructure is complemented with intrusion detection systems (IDS) with a layer of blockchain. Anomalies identified and verified attack signatures are aggregated to standardized intelligence packages and distributed over smart contracts. These agreements specify participation, validation and automatic response triggers, providing trustless coordination and near-simultaneous threat intelligence propagation throughout the network. Low-latency optimized consensus algorithms guarantee intelligence is quickly validated and disseminated, while cryptographic access controls protect mission critical operational data. The proposed research focuses on the following important issues, such as privacy- preserving intelligence sharing, robust data poisoning attacks, and resource-efficient operation for constrained computing resources. A hybrid on-chain/of f-chain approach is introduced to reconcile transparency with scalability by on-chaining active threat hashes and metadata at the same time, resting bulk data in encrypted decentralized storage. With this solution, blockchain bloat is avoided while retaining the ability to verify the authenticity of shared intelligence. Experimental verification with simulated cyberattacks in a multi-node testbed confirms that the proposed architecture reduces threat detection-to-response delay, enhances global situational awareness and avoids redundant investigations. Moreover, the auditability of blockchain of fers an immutable ledger of incidents which can be used for forensic analysis and compliance reporting with no overhead. The results show encourage that blockchain-based decentralized IDS can revolutionize collaborative security of critical infrastructure systems including power, water, transportation, and medical facilities. The platform, which, due to its reliance on cryptographic trust, forges from heterogeneous providers a coordinated and resilient Software solution that automates, transparently demarcates and disinter-mediates CTI sharing can result in cyber security through collective defense β€“ suspected adversaries during one period may have evolved to be allies to each other during another period. Β©2025 IEEE.},
