	abstract = {The rapid growth of electronic health systems has significantly transformed healthcare by digitizing patient records into Electronic Health Records (EHRs), thereby improving clinical efficiency, accessibility, and real-time disease monitoring. Despite these advancements, existing health record exchange systems face persistent challenges, including privacy breaches, limited interoperability, high storage overhead, and scalability constraints. Addressing these issues requires a comprehensive solution that ensures both robust security and seamless data sharing across healthcare environments. This study introduces a blockchain-enabled, privacy-preserving framework that integrates advanced cryptographic methods such as ciphertext-policy attribute-based encryption (CP-ABE), federated learning, and a hybrid blockchain architecture. The design emphasizes fine-grained, patient-centric access control, safeguarding sensitive health information while employing smart contracts for automated policy enforcement and compliance monitoring. To validate its effectiveness, comparative analyses were conducted against state-of-the-art models, including lightweight encryption with federated learning, Blowfish-based blockchain, IPFS-enabled HealthRecChain, and homomorphic encryption techniques. Performance evaluation covered twelve key parameters, including privacy preservation, scalability, latency, computational efficiency, encryption speed, storage utilization, interoperability, and auditability. Experimental results show that the proposed framework delivers superior outcomes, achieving 96% data privacy, supporting up to 1200 transactions per second (TPS), reducing latency to 180 ms, and providing enhanced brute-force resistance with a score of 9.5 / 10. Additionally, it demonstrated improved efficiency in encryption/decryption, key management, and secure data exchange across heterogeneous systems. In conclusion, the framework offers a scalable, secure, and ethically aligned solution for health record exchange. By balancing decentralization, high performance, and patientcentered governance, it lays a strong foundation for nextgeneration smart healthcare ecosystems. Β© 2025 IEEE.},
