	affiliations = {Ibm, United States},
	abstract = {Ensuring the authenticity and origin of Linux image builds has become essential for keeping today's software supply chains secure. Securing keys which are part of Linux image during the build process is crucial for protecting sensitive data and ensuring compliance with privacy standards such as the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR). Traditional build systems protect individual keys but provide no cryptographic continuity or auditability between them. This paper presents a new and secure block chain inspired system that employs encrypted key management alongside secure build environments where the keys used in the build process and the keys inserted to the Linux image are cryptographically linked to its predecessor. To minimize the risk of key leakage, key rotation and environment isolation are implemented to enforce strict access controls. The resulting chained key lineage embedded to the Linux image can be verified for authenticity and provenance without relying on external trust anchors. The proposed approach enhances trust; accountability and compliance in software build lifecycle providing a robust foundation for maintaining the keys. Β© 2025 IEEE.},
