	affiliations = {Department of Computing and Information Technologies, RIT Kosovo (A.U.K), Rochester Institute of Technology β€“ RIT Global Campus, Prishtina, Kosovo},
	abstract = {In this paper, we propose a lightweight steganography scheme using the Laplacian mathematical model to secure and privacy-preserving communication for edge computing. The contribution proposed in this paper is to meet the challenges of computing overheads associated with the classical cryptographic systems, as well as poor robustness and adaptability for traditional steganographic methods like LSB (Least Significant Bit), DCT (Discrete Cosine Transform), DWT (Discrete Wavelet Transform) and SVD (Singular Value Decomposition). The goal is to build an efficient Laplacian-based embedding model for imperceptible and secure data hiding in low-resource, distributed systems.Β The Graph-Laplacian operator is utilized to learn the local pixel relations and hide secrets in the high-frequency structural components of the cover image. Lightweight key-controlled embedding scheme provides security and integrity of data, while the modular framework is very flexible to apply over different categories of media such as images, audio, and text. The system is lightweight and efficient for edge devices including Raspberry Pi and Jetson Nano, with low consumption of memory and fast processing.Β Experiments conducted on standard 512Γ— 512 grayscale benchmark images with a binary β€BLOCKCHAINβ€ watermark show that the proposed method achieves a PSNR of 40.7 dB, SSIM of 0.986, and entropy deviation (Ξ”H) of 0.017. Compared with LSB-, DCT-, DWT-, SVD-, and hybrid DWTβ€“SVD-based schemes, it delivers improved imperceptibility, efficiency, and robustness (BER β‰¤ 2.4%, CC β‰¥ 0.98).Β The framework provides a practical, secure, and lightweight solution for privacy-preserving data transmission in IoT and decentralized applications. Β© The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer Nature Singapore Pte Ltd. 2026.},
	author_keywords = {Chebyshevβ€™s inequality; Edge computing; Feature embedding; Graph signal processing; Information hiding; KL divergence; Laplacian steganography; Lightweight cryptography; Privacy preservation},
