	abstract = {The issue of secured data transmission in decentralized and distributed systems is still a challenge that needs to be solved in order to provide privacy of the individual as well as the integrity and security of the data. This makes traditional centralized approaches vulnerable to sensitive information leaks and unauthorized access, thus warranting investigation of more alternative privacy-preserving methodologies. In this paper, we propose a novel secure & privacy-preserving data transmission framework by integrating the advantage of federated learning and the benefit of blockchain technology. This means that data stays on the device and is not shared with the central vote processor, all but eliminating the risks of data centralisation. The incorporation of blockchain technology adds an extra layer of data security with its immutable and transparent nature, along with decentralized consensus algorithms, which prevent unauthorized changes and build trust among entities involved. We present a new framework that utilizes smart contracts to automate the process of regulating data access and enforcing privacy-protective policies while guaranteeing compliance with data governance policies. Furthermore, cryptographic approaches like homomorphic encryption or differential privacy have been integrated into these methodologies to protect data confidentiality and allow secure collaborative learning over distributed nodes. It leverages an optimized consensus algorithm to strike a balance between security and computational efficiency, alleviating latency concerns often related to blockchain solutions. Moreover, experimental assessments validate that the unification of federated learning - supported by blockchain - enhances data protection without compromising model accuracy and scalability over different networks. Results show that this method can both mitigate the risks of centralizing data storage and reduce vulnerability to adversarial attacks, while still keeping people's individual privacy intact without degrading system performance.  Β© 2025 IEEE.},
