	affiliations = {Department of Information Technology, College of Computer, Qassim University, Buraydah, Saudi Arabia; Department of Computer Science, Faculty of Sciences of Gafsa, University of Gafsa, Gafsa, Tunisia},
	abstract = {The fast growth of the Internet of Medical things (IoMT) and its connection to cloud-computing environments have led to a major risk of exposing sensitive medical information to security and privacy risks in the distributed healthcare environment. The majority of the currently available cloud-assisted IoMT systems use mechanisms of cloud-single session authentication, in which trust is instilled once and long-term access privileges remain in effect during the session. These types of fixed trust models can be vulnerable to session hijacking, node fault, insider abuse, and dynamic context, and therefore endangering patient privacy and regulatory adherence. To address these shortcomings, in this paper, a network-based security architecture is introduced: BioTwinGuard is a Personal Medical Digital Twin (PMDT) in combination with a Continuous Trust Posture (CTP) scheme. The PMDT, installed on the local device of the patient, constantly checks the contextual cues of trust, such as: the role of the clinician, the integrity of the device, consent state, and consistency of behavior, and makes access decisions dynamically. According to this analysis, the framework creates Just-in-Time Re-Encryption Capabilities (JIT-REC) which creates encrypted medical information with fine-grained, time-limited, and revocable access. In contrast to all other existing persistent delegation schemes, JIT-REC provides cryptographic privileges with a temporary and self-adaptive lifecycle, and adjusts it to real-time changes in trust. The suggested scheme takes advantage of the lightweight elliptic-curve cryptography (ECC) to guarantee anonymity, forward secrecy, and energy-efficient functionality that is appropriate in resource-constrained IoMT devices. Formal security analysis proves the proposed frameworkβ€™s resistance to replay, impersonation and key-exposure attacks under DY and CK adversarial models. The evaluation of the performance shows that BioTwinGuard can sustain a lightweight computational profile and implement continuous trust enforcement at the cost of a 25%-30% performance overhead over the baseline ECC schemes. Energy usage is lower than (Formula presented) per trust cycle while energy usage of authentication processes is lower by up to 23% of the non-optimized protocols. BioTwinGuard creates an open and scalable, resilient and patient-sovereign paradigm of security, allowing adaptive consent administration and real-time access revocation of next-generation IoMT ecosystems. Β© 2026 Elsevier B.V. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.},
	author_keywords = {6G security; Blockchain; Federated learning; Fuzzy logic; Privacy preservation; Trust management},
	keywords = {Authentication; Cloud computing; Data privacy; Medical computing; Medical information systems; Network security; Trusted computing; 6g security; Block-chain; Curve cryptography; Elliptic curve; Fuzzy-Logic; Just-in-time; Medical information; Privacy preservation; Reencryptions; Trust management; Public key cryptography},
	correspondence_address = {S. El Khediri; Department of Information Technology, College of Computer, Qassim University, Buraydah, Saudi Arabia; email: s.elkhediri@qu.edu.sa},
	publisher = {Elsevier B.V.},
