	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105035530747?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Psg Polytechnic College, India; Psg College Technology, India},
	abstract = {Urban water utilities face persistent challenges in ensuring fair billing, leak detection, and transparent governance under growing demand fluctuations. Traditional static tariff systems and centralized data repositories often result in inefficiencies, hidden losses, and limited trust. This study presents a novel AI-IoT-Blockchain integration framework that seamlessly integrates LoRaWAN-based sensing, Gradient Boosted Random Forest (GBRF) forecasting, and cost-efficient IPFS-blockchain anchoring to enable adaptive and transparent tariffing in urban water systems, thereby ensuring scalability, trust, and transparency in realtime water management.Household-level telemetry is collected through low-power LoRaWAN meters and processed in real time for short-term demand prediction and anomaly detection. Forecast outputs dynamically feed an automated tariff engine, with tariff schedules and cryptographic anchors committed to a public blockchain, ensuring tamper-resistant and auditable billing. A prototype deployment demonstrates that the proposed system achieves forecasting accuracy with RMSE β‰ 3.1% and detects 92% of simulated leaks with <6% false positives. Furthermore, hybrid IPFS-blockchain anchoring reduces costs by βΌ 90% while maintaining auditability and data integrity. A dashboard prototype illustrates operator and consumer transparency, bridging technical feasibility with civic trust. This study highlights a practical path for deploying scalable, secure, and adaptive water management systems, contributing to smarter and more resilient cities.  Β© 2025 IEEE.},
