	abstract = {The rapid growth of global tourism has created significant challenges for sustainable resource management, particularly in UNESCO World Heritage Sites. Current centralized decision-making systems often fail to address the fragmented nature of tourism data, leading to inefficiencies in resource allocation and privacy concerns. This study proposes a novel hybrid architecture that integrates federated learning (FL) with blockchain technology to enable privacy-preserving, decentralized decision-making in tourism ecosystems. Homomorphic encryption is employed to secure model aggregation, ensuring 128-bit confidentiality against adversarial attacks. The system utilizes GPU-Accelerated cryptographic operations to reduce computational latency by 43% compared to conventional CPU-based implementations. A dynamic resource matching algorithm, supported by reinforcement learning, achieves Pareto optimality in 92.4% of tested scenarios, significantly improving resource utilization. Experimental validation at UNESCO World Heritage Sites demonstrates a 23.7% increase in resource efficiency, while privacy leakage probability is reduced to less than 10-5. Additionally, the blockchain-based consensus mechanism achieves 98.3% accuracy under Byzantine attack scenarios, ensuring trust and system robustness. By aligning intelligent decision-making frameworks with sustainable tourism objectives, this research provides a scalable, secure, and efficient solution for real-Time resource management. The proposed architecture holds potential for broader applications in other resource-intensive domains, such as smart cities and maritime ecosystems, where privacy-preserving and decentralized decision-making are critical.  Β© COPYRIGHT SPIE. Downloading of the abstract is permitted for personal use only.},
