	abstract = {Integrity and security of computer evidence is very important in the evolving face of computer forensics. The case study shows a Blockchain-Based Forensic Framework, which is founded on the Practical Byzantine Fault Tolerance (PBFT) to implement an immutable log of the forensics and Threshold Cryptography to store data securely. A model that is based on AI-powered integrity verification with an optimized XGBoost classifier has a detection rate of 98%. It uses SMOTE to balance the dataset and feature engineering to improve the classification accuracy. The solidness of the framework is proved by the experimental outcomes which show the better results in demonstrating the tampered evidence. The framework is dealing with the severe problem of ensuring a verifiable chain of custody. The cryptographic security and the impossibility of its modification of forensic logs offered by the introduction of blockchain gives an auditorial track. The consensus provided by PBFT avoids potentially critical nodes. Threshold Cryptography shares keys to decrypted messages so as to reduce unauthorized users. The AI-verification with the assistance of SMOTE and feature engineering identifies the minor disorder in a significantly better way. The presented synergistic solution is a strong and scalable method of ensuring digital evidence in order to increase the credibility of cybercrime investigation.  Β© 2025 IEEE.},
