	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105031583474?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {The British University in Dubai, Faculty of Engineering & Information, Dubai, United Arab Emirates; The British University in Dubai, Dubai, United Arab Emirates; Mutah University, College of Engineering, Department of Computer Engineering, Jordan; Higher Colleges of Technology, Al Ain City, Abu Dhabi, United Arab Emirates; Computer Science Department, Faculty of Computers and Artificial Intelligence, Damietta University, Damietta, Egypt; Faculty of Computer Science and Engineering, New Mansoura University, Dakhlia, Egypt; The British University in Dubai, Department of Computer Science, Dubai, United Arab Emirates; Hourani Center for Applied Scientific Research, Al-Ahliyya Amman University, Department of Networks and Cybersecurity, Amman, Jordan},
	abstract = {In the digital transformation of judicial systems, the secure and tamper-proof storage of legal documents is critical for maintaining transparency and trust. Leveraging blockchain technology, particularly Hyperledger Fabric, presents a promising approach to ensure document integrity in digital courts. However, existing systems often rely on centralized databases, which are vulnerable to unauthorized access, data tampering, and a lack of verifiable audit trails. These limitations compromise the authenticity and accountability of sensitive legal records. To address these issues, this paper proposes L-CHAIN (Legal Court Hyperledger-based Architecture for Immutable Notarization) a blockchain-based framework utilizing the Hyperledger Fabric platform. L-CHAIN employs a permissioned blockchain to log cryptographic hashes of legal documents, while the documents themselves are stored off-chain. Smart contracts (also known as chaincode) are used for access control, timestamped registration, and automated integrity verification. The use of Fabric's Membership Service Provider (MSP) ensures role-based access for judges, lawyers, and clerks, enforcing a secure and auditable environment. The proposed method is applied in an E-Filing System for Court Documents, where legal professionals securely submit and retrieve case files. The system guarantees immutability, verifiability, and traceability for every document interaction through decentralized consensus and hash validation. The findings demonstrate that L-CHAIN effectively mitigates data tampering risks, enforces role-based access, and creates a transparent audit trail for the handling of legal documents. Overall, the proposed framework enhances the reliability and integrity of digital court systems, laying the foundation for a more secure and accountable judicial process.  Β© 2025 IEEE.},
