	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105032737361?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {MLR Institute of Technology, Department of Information Technology, Hyderabad, India},
	abstract = {The work introduces a new Digital Forensic Investigation (DFI) model that will make digital evidence confidentiality, integrity, and authenticity stronger due to linking blockchain technology and secure cryptographic algorithms. With the help of the Rijndael encryption algorithm (AES) and Secure Hash Algorithm (SHA), the model is used to safeguard sensitive information that is generated in the line of forensic explorations. AES encryption works through four stages i.e. Sub Bytes, Shift Rows, Mix Columns and Add Round Key to make forensic reports of different law enforcement departments safe and sound. Also, hashing based on SHA checks the data integrity, where it converts the input data into insecure, unintelligible format. The system is divided into a number of modules, such as the Application Manager, Police Station, Forensic Staff, Pathology Lab and Doctor, all linked to permissioned blockchain network. Based on the Hyperledger Fabric architecture, the model provides less-privileged access and management to the evidence, with evidence handling leaving an unchangeable record of evidence handover. The model is based on a cloud-based stack programmed using Ubuntu 18.04 LTS and a Steam Cloud system which exposes an EBS instance to store the huge storage halt and it has shown promising outcomes in securing the digital evidence. The results indicate that DFI model is competent enough to fulfill the high requirements of forensic investigation process to guarantee the integrity and unforged nature of digital evidence.  Β© 2025 IEEE.},
