	affiliations = {King Khalid University, College of Computer Science, Department of Informatics and Computer Systems, Abha, 61421, Saudi Arabia},
	abstract = {When devices are isolated after a suspected compromise, investigators can no longer perform secure remote forensics. Current approaches lack immutable logging, multi-party authorization, and cryptographic verification, making it challenging to maintain evidence integrity and chain of custody during incident response. We present BMFCP (Blockchain-Mediated Forensic Communication Protocol), a system for applying secure forensic investigations on isolated devices using blockchain coordination. BMFCP uses smart contracts to manage device isolation states and enforce multi-party authorization, requiring approval from multiple organizations before executing sensitive forensic actions. Agents submit cryptographically signed execution proofs, and all evidence is verified through hashes recorded on the blockchain. Every action is logged with timestamps and digital signatures, creating a complete chain of custody. In this paper, we built a prototype on Hyperledger Fabric. We tested our method with 2 and 3 organizations. Results show transaction latencies of 1-3 seconds and throughput around 4 transactions per second, which is sufficient for forensic work. Our security analysis shows that BMFCP prevents evidence tampering, enforces multi-party oversight, and creates audit trails. Β© 2026 The Authors.},
