	affiliations = {Amity Institute of Information Technology Amity University, Noida, India; New Delhi Institute of Management, Delhi, India},
	abstract = {Blockchain and Software-Defined Networking (SDN) are emerging as collective technologies delivering support that promotes higher security, reliability, and network performance. Blockchain is closely connected with security paradigm. Blockchain popularly known for its financial applications, is a distributed ledger that promises immutability and transparency of data transactions. Alternatively, SDN is an infrastructure that dissociates the network control plane from hardware architecture, thus centralising network surveillance, facilitating smooth resource distribution, and bringing down operational overhead. Utilizing blockchain cryptographic algorithms will aid SDN in protecting control plane communication by eliminating the possibility of any cyber threat. Blockchain smart contracts can execute automatically among various SDN systems, including service agents and clients. Distributed functionality of blockchain guarantees that network infrastructure does not completely rely on a single point of failure. Distributing network control between numerous nodes, blockchain have the ability to scale up network traffic in SDN. SDN infrastructure can gain an unparalleled degree of trust and security by leveraging blockchain decentralised ledger. Blockchain keep record of data transaction and network configuration alteration in a timestamp manner. This plays a pivotal role in controlling unauthorised access within the network and any malicious activity is detected instantly. SDN simplifies smart traffic routing decisions in real time, enhancing the authenticity and competency of data transmitted in the blockchain. SDN assists in automatic switching and rerouting techniques during failover, that improves blockchain resilience against connectivity outage. Intersection of Blockchain and SDN have triggered the arrival of innovative solutions to overcome the challenges that exist in several domains. For achieving seamless association of these leading-edge technologies requires adopting present SDN techniques to make room for blockchain protocols, which can turn out to be complicated and time-consuming at the same time. The convergence of SDN and blockchain has enormous potential to transform network operations and data security. This chapter deals with an overview of principles behind blockchain and SDN, analysis of literature work done so far in this domain, examining the challenges of centralised SDN that are prone to cyber-attacks, convergence of blockchain with SDN, emerging case studies, practical deployment for blockchain, roadblocks in deployment of blockchain with SDN, conclusion and future scope. Β© 2026 selection and editorial matter, Sarvesh Tanwar, Sumit Badotra and Harish Garg; individual chapters, the contributors.},
	keywords = {Computer crime; Cryptography; Distributed computer systems; Network architecture; Network security; Software reliability; Block-chain; Data transaction; Financial applications; High reliability; High securities; Higher networks; Network control plane; Performance; Security paradigms; Software-defined networkings; Blockchain},
