	abstract = {The global fishery supply chain still suffers from opaque traceability, inconsistent data, and weak quality enforcement, leading to frequent fraud and compliance issues. This study presents a blockchain-driven traceability and quality-assured procurement model that enables trusted, event-driven automation across fisheries. A consortium blockchain with three smart contracts (QualitySLA, EscrowPayment, ProcurementTrigger) verifies laboratory and IoT data, automates conditional settlements, and initiates procurement decisions. The architecture integrates four layers: data capture, blockchain consortium, smart contract automation, and enterprise system integration. Each transaction in the chain-from fishing and processing to quality validation and distribution-is recorded as immutable evidence. The implemented proof-of-concept (PoC) demonstrated stable performance under 200 transaction iterations, with average execution times below 100 ms and consistent gas efficiency across core operations. Results confirm that blockchain-based automation enforces deterministic business rules and minimizes disputes through cryptographic verification rather than relying on predictive AI or centralized authorities. The model strengthens trust, transparency, and sustainability across multistakeholder fisheries, making blockchain a viable foundation for digital transformation in the maritime economy. Β© 2025 IEEE.},
