	affiliations = {Department of Economics and Finance, University of the Free State, Bloemfontein, South Africa; Department of Applied Informatics, Walter Sisulu University, East London, South Africa},
	abstract = {This systematic review addresses spectrum under-utilization caused by fixed allocation policies by presenting a taxonomy of spectrum sensing techniques and consolidating evidence on their performance and security in cognitive radio and emerging 5G/6G networks. Using a PRISMA-guided search, screening, and eligibility procedure, 6,513 records were retrieved from the Web of Science and Scopus and filtered to a final corpus of 1,659 peer-reviewed studies published between 2019 and 2024. The selected studies were analyzed using bibliometric mapping (keyword co-occurrence, co-citation, and co-authorship) in VOSviewer and a structured qualitative synthesis to answer two questions: (i) which sensing categories dominate recent research, and (ii) what strengths, limitations, and security risks characterize each category. The analysis yields a four-branch taxonomy; primary-user detection, spectrum sharing, machine-learningbased sensing, and hybrid approaches and reveals five dominant thematic clusters that reflect a clear shift toward data-driven detection and cooperative decision fusion. Evidence across the reviewed studies shows that centralized cooperative schemes can achieve high detection probability but may struggle with scalability and reporting overhead, whereas decentralized consensus protocols improve robustness and fault tolerance but require careful coordination to limit latency. Machine-learning and hybrid methods generally improve performance at low SNR, yet practical deployment is constrained by computational complexity, dependence on labeled data, and limited SDR/testbed validation. Security threats, particularly primary-user emulation and false-data injection, are frequently mitigated through cooperative sensing coupled with cryptographic primitives and blockchain-based trust mechanisms. The review concludes with implications and research directions, emphasizing ultra-lightweight models, secure cooperative protocols, reproducible benchmarks, and more real-world experimentation in practice. Β© 2026, University of Bahrain. All rights reserved.},
