	abstract = {Worldwide Coronavirus Disease 2019 (COVID-19) has infected more than 220 million people and caused more than 4.5 million deaths and it is increasing daily. Precisely, the United States has become the country with the largest number of known infections with more than 40 million cases and caused more than 0.65 million deaths and even increasing. Contact tracing is an effective disease control strategy for highly infectious diseases such as H1N1 and more recently the novel COVID-19. It involves collecting information about infectious patients and their contacts for restricting the further transmission of disease. This data is also extremely valuable for the city/state administrator as it can be used to localize active infection areas and issue public warnings. Despite the effectiveness of this mechanism, there are serious concerns regarding user data privacy and tracking system efficacy. To address these shortcomings, we are aiming towards building a blockchain-based decentralized contract tracing solution to strengthen infectious disease mitigation approaches. Blockchain functions as a distributed ledger using advanced cryptographic techniques and ensures that the data remains immutable and auditable. The main objective of our current work is to build a prototype tracking system by collecting, matching, and processing personal location-aware information about infected persons and their contacts. We are also evaluating our solution with varied population size and time to demonstrate efficacy and efficiency. Our proposed tracking system visualizes simulated infection spreads, based on varied infection and recovery rates. The proposed solution will help healthcare administrators to proactively manage mild to highly catastrophic pandemic situations. Overall, this study will help create a strategic road map in developing a secured digital contact tracing solution to mitigate highly communicable diseases.  Β© 2021 IEEE.},
